dart-oop:
- Class

    **Dart class modifiers** (introduced in Dart 3).

    | **Modifier** | **Can instantiate?** | **Can extend outside library?** | **Can implement outside library?** | **Can extend inside library?** | **Can implement inside library?** |
    | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
    | **none** | Yes | Yes | Yes | **Yes** | **Yes** |
    | **abstract** | No | Yes | Yes | **Yes** | **Yes** |
    | **base** | Yes | Yes (must be base/final/sealed) | No | **Yes**(must be base/final/sealed) | **Yes**(must be base/final/sealed) |
    | **interface** | Yes | No | Yes | **Yes** | **Yes** |
    | **final** | Yes | No | No | **Yes** (base/final/sealed subtype) | **Yes** (base/final/sealed subtype) |
    | **sealed** | No | No | No | **Yes** | **Yes** |

    ## 🔹 `none` (no modifier)

    **Full freedom** — anyone can do anything with it.

    ```dart
    class Animal {
      void breathe() => print("Breathing...");
    }

    // ✅ Instantiate
    var a = Animal();

    // ✅ Extend
    class Dog extends Animal {}

    // ✅ Implement
    class Robot implements Animal {
      void breathe() => print("Simulating breath");
    }
    ```

    ---

    ## 🔹 `abstract`

    **Can't instantiate**, but can be extended or implemented. Used as a blueprint.

    ```dart
    abstract class Shape {
      double area(); // no body required
    }

    // ❌ var s = Shape();  → ERROR

    // ✅ Extend
    class Circle extends Shape {
      double area() => 3.14 * 5 * 5;
    }

    // ✅ Implement
    class Square implements Shape {
      double area() => 4 * 4;
    }
    ```

    ---

    ## 🔹 `base`

    **Can instantiate and extend**, but **cannot be implemented** outside the library. Protects internal logic from being "faked" via `implements`.

    ```dart
    // lib/animal.dart
    base class Animal {
      void breathe() => print("Breathing...");
    }
    ```

    ```dart
    // main.dart
    import 'animal.dart';

    // ✅ Instantiate
    var a = Animal();

    // ✅ Extend (but subclass must also be base/final/sealed)
    base class Dog extends Animal {}

    // ❌ Implement → ERROR
    class Robot implements Animal { ... }
    ```

    > The key rule: if you extend a `base` class, your subclass **must also** be `base`, `final`, or `sealed`.
    > 

    ---

    ## 🔹 `interface`

    **Can instantiate**, but **cannot be extended** outside the library — only implemented.

    ```dart
    // lib/logger.dart
    interface class Logger {
      void log(String msg) => print(msg);
    }
    ```

    ```dart
    // main.dart
    import 'logger.dart';

    // ✅ Instantiate
    var l = Logger();

    // ❌ Extend → ERROR
    class FileLogger extends Logger { ... }

    // ✅ Implement (must define everything yourself)
    class FileLogger implements Logger {
      void log(String msg) => print("File: $msg");
    }
    ```

    > This is useful for **API design** — you want users to follow your contract, not inherit your internals.
    > 

    ---

    ## 🔹 `final`

    **Can instantiate**, but **no extending, no implementing** outside the library. Completely locked down for inheritance.

    ```dart
    // lib/token.dart
    final class AuthToken {
      final String value;
      AuthToken(this.value);
    }
    ```

    ```dart
    // main.dart
    import 'token.dart';

    // ✅ Instantiate
    var token = AuthToken("abc123");

    // ❌ Extend  → ERROR
    // ❌ Implement → ERROR
    ```

    > Use this when the class is a **complete, self-contained unit** and inheritance would break its guarantees (e.g., security tokens, value objects).
    > 

    ---

    ## 🔹 `sealed`

    **Cannot instantiate, extend, or implement** outside the library. But inside the library, all direct subtypes are known — enabling **exhaustive pattern matching**.

    ```dart
    // lib/result.dart
    sealed class Result {}

    class Success extends Result { final String data; Success(this.data); }
    class Failure extends Result { final String error; Failure(this.error); }
    ```

    ```dart
    // main.dart
    Result r = Success("Done!");

    // ✅ Exhaustive switch — Dart knows ALL possible subtypes
    switch (r) {
      case Success s:
        print("✅ ${s.data}");
      case Failure f:
        print("❌ ${f.error}");
      // No default needed — compiler is satisfied
    }
    ```

    > `sealed` is perfect for **union types / state modeling** (like `Success | Failure | Loading`).
    > 

    ---

    ## Quick Mental Model

    | Modifier | Think of it as... |
    | --- | --- |
    | `none` | A regular public class |
    | `abstract` | A blueprint you can't build directly |
    | `base` | "Extend me, but don't fake me" |
    | `interface` | "Use my contract, not my code" |
    | `final` | "Use me as-is, I'm done" |
    | `sealed` | "I own all my subtypes" |
- Abstraction

    ```dart
    abstract class Animal {
      void speak();
    }

    class Dog extends Animal {
      @override
      void speak() {
        print("Bark!");
      }
    }

    void main() {
    // Animal a = Animal(); 
    // ❌ Error: cannot instantiate abstract class
      Dog d = Dog();
      d.speak(); // Bark!
    }
    ```

- Encapsulation

    ```dart
    class BankAccount {

      double _balance = 0;

      // Public getter
      double get balance => _balance;

      void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
          _balance += amount;
        }
      }
    }
    ```

- Inheritance

    ```dart
    class Vehicle {
      void drive() {
        print("Driving vehicle");
      }
    }

    class Car extends Vehicle {
      @override
      void drive() {
        print("Driving car");
      }
    }

    void main() {
      Car myCar = Car();
      myCar.drive(); // Prints: Driving car
    }
    ```

- Polymorphism

    ```text
    Polymorphism in Dart
    - A common interface can have multiple implementations.
    - Method overrides are selected for the runtime object.
    - Selected operators can be implemented as instance methods.
    - Same-name methods cannot be overloaded by parameter signature.
    ```

### Dart-specific details

- An underscore makes a member private to its **library**, not to a single class.
- Dart supports method overriding and selected operator overloads, but not multiple methods with the same name and different parameter signatures.
- A subtype of a `base` or `final` class must itself be `base`, `final`, or `sealed`, including within the defining library.
- `sealed` cannot be directly instantiated anywhere. Its direct subtypes are restricted to the defining library.

dart-generics:
## 1. What is Generic?

Generic means **reusable + type-safe** code.

```dart
List<String> names = ['Amin', 'Rahim'];
List<int> numbers = [1, 2, 3];
```

`List<T>` can work with any type.

---

## 2. Generic Model

```dart
class ApiResponse<T> {
  final bool success;
  final String message;
  final T? data;

  const ApiResponse({
    required this.success,
    required this.message,
    this.data,
  });
}
```

Usage:

```dart
final userResponse = ApiResponse<User>(
  success: true,
  message: 'User loaded',
  data: user,
);

final productResponse = ApiResponse<Product>(
  success: true,
  message: 'Product loaded',
  data: product,
);
```

Same model, different data types.

---

## 3. Generic Function

```dart
T getFirst<T>(List<T> items) {
  return items.first;
}
```

Usage:

```dart
final name = getFirst<String>(['A', 'B']);
final number = getFirst<int>([10, 20]);
```

---

## 4. Generic Service

```dart
abstract class BaseRepository<T> {
  Future<List<T>> getAll();
  Future<T> getById(int id);
}
```

Usage:

```dart
class UserRepository implements BaseRepository<User> {
  @override
  Future<List<User>> getAll() async {
    return [];
  }

  @override
  Future<User> getById(int id) async {
    throw UnimplementedError();
  }
}
```

---

## 5. Generic with Constraint

Use `extends` when generic type must follow a rule.

```dart
class CacheService<T extends Object> {
  final Map<String, T> _cache = {};

  void save(String key, T value) {
    _cache[key] = value;
  }

  T? get(String key) {
    return _cache[key];
  }
}
```

---

## 6. Common Generic Letters

```dart
T  // Type
E  // Element
K  // Key
V  // Value
```

Example:

```dart
Map<String, int> scores = {
  'Math': 90,
};
```

Here:

```dart
K = String
V = int
```

---

## 7. Avoid Dynamic

Avoid:

```dart
ApiResponse data;
List items;
```

Better:

```dart
ApiResponse<User> data;
List<User> items;
```

Because generics give **compile-time safety**.

---

## Quick Memory

```dart
List<T>              // reusable list
ApiResponse<T>       // reusable API model
BaseRepository<T>    // reusable service
Map<K, V>            // key-value generic
T extends Object     // restricted generic
```

Main idea:

```dart
Generics = reusable code without losing type safety
```

dart-extensions:
## 1. What is Extension?

Extension means you can **add new methods/getters** to existing classes without modifying them.

```dart
extension StringExtension on String {
  bool get isValidEmail {
    return RegExp(r'^[^@]+@[^@]+\.[^@]+$').hasMatch(this);
  }
}
```

Usage:

```dart
final email = 'test@gmail.com';

print(email.isValidEmail); // true
```

---

## 2. String Extension

```dart
extension StringExtension on String {
  String get capitalize {
    if (isEmpty) return this;
    return '${this[0].toUpperCase()}${substring(1)}';
  }

  bool get isBlank => trim().isEmpty;
}
```

Usage:

```dart
print('hello'.capitalize); // Hello
print('   '.isBlank);      // true
```

---

## 3. Nullable String Extension

```dart
extension NullableStringExtension on String? {
  bool get isNullOrEmpty {
    return this == null || this!.trim().isEmpty;
  }
}
```

Usage:

```dart
String? name;

print(name.isNullOrEmpty); // true
```

---

## 4. BuildContext Extension

Very useful in Flutter UI.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

extension ContextExtension on BuildContext {
  Size get screenSize => MediaQuery.sizeOf(this);

  double get screenWidth => screenSize.width;

  double get screenHeight => screenSize.height;

  ThemeData get theme => Theme.of(this);

  TextTheme get textTheme => theme.textTheme;
}
```

Usage:

```dart
final width = context.screenWidth;
final titleStyle = context.textTheme.titleLarge;
```

---

## 5. DateTime Extension

```dart
extension DateTimeExtension on DateTime {
  bool get isToday {
    final now = DateTime.now();

    return year == now.year &&
        month == now.month &&
        day == now.day;
  }
}
```

Usage:

```dart
final date = DateTime.now();

print(date.isToday); // true
```

---

## 6. List Extension

```dart
extension ListExtension<T> on List<T> {
  T? get firstOrNull {
    if (isEmpty) return null;
    return first;
  }
}
```

Usage:

```dart
final numbers = <int>[];

print(numbers.firstOrNull); // null
```

---

## 7. Best Practice

Good:

```dart
context.screenWidth
'hello'.capitalize
date.isToday
list.firstOrNull
```

Avoid making extensions too magical:

```dart
context.goToHomePage(); // not always good
```

Keep extensions for **small reusable helpers**, not big business logic.

---

## Quick Memory

```dart
extension ExtensionName on ExistingClass {
  ReturnType get propertyName => value;

  ReturnType methodName() {
    return value;
  }
}
```

Main idea:

```dart
Extensions = add helper methods to existing classes safely
```

### SDK and validation notes

Dart already provides `Iterable.firstOrNull`; the custom list extension above demonstrates syntax and is not needed in production. Email regular expressions here are simple format examples, not proof that an address exists. Extension methods use the receiver’s static type and do not modify the original class.

dart-function-types:
## 1. Function Type

A function type describes:

```dart
ReturnType Function(ParameterType value)
```

Example:

```dart
String? Function(String? value)
```

Means:

```dart
takes String?
returns String?
```

Used for validators:

```dart
validator: (value) {
  if (value == null || value.isEmpty) {
    return 'Required';
  }
  return null;
}
```

---

## 2. Typedef

`typedef` gives a function type a clean name.

```dart
typedef FieldValidator = String? Function(String? value);
```

Now instead of writing:

```dart
final String? Function(String?) validator;
```

Write:

```dart
final FieldValidator validator;
```

Cleaner and reusable.

---

## 3. Common Flutter Callback Types

```dart
VoidCallback        // void Function()
ValueChanged<T>     // void Function(T value)
ValueGetter<T>      // T Function()
ValueSetter<T>      // void Function(T value)
AsyncCallback       // Future<void> Function()
```

Example:

```dart
final VoidCallback? onTap;
final ValueChanged<String>? onChanged;
```

---

## 4. Button Callback

```dart
class CustomButton extends StatelessWidget {
  const CustomButton({
    super.key,
    required this.onPressed,
  });

  final VoidCallback? onPressed;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return ElevatedButton(
      onPressed: onPressed,
      child: const Text('Submit'),
    );
  }
}
```

Usage:

```dart
CustomButton(
  onPressed: () {
    print('Clicked');
  },
)
```

---

## 5. Validator Typedef

```dart
typedef FieldValidator = String? Function(String? value);
```

Example:

```dart
FieldValidator requiredField(String fieldName) {
  return (value) {
    if (value == null || value.trim().isEmpty) {
      return '$fieldName is required';
    }
    return null;
  };
}
```

Usage:

```dart
TextFormField(
  validator: requiredField('Email'),
)
```

---

## 6. Async Callback

Use this for API calls, login, Bluetooth connect, etc.

```dart
typedef AsyncCallback = Future<void> Function();
```

Example:

```dart
final Future<void> Function()? onPressed;
```

Or custom:

```dart
typedef AsyncButtonCallback = Future<void> Function();
```

Usage:

```dart
AsyncButtonCallback login = () async {
  await Future.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 1));
};
```

---

## 7. Avoid broad Function types

Avoid:

```dart
final Function onTap;
```

Better:

```dart
final VoidCallback onTap;
```

Avoid:

```dart
final Function(String) onChanged;
```

Better:

```dart
final ValueChanged<String> onChanged;
```

Because specific function types are safer and easier to understand.

---

## 8. Quick Memory

```dart
VoidCallback
// no input, no return

ValueChanged<String>
// takes String, returns void

String? Function(String?)
// takes nullable String, returns nullable String

Future<void> Function()
// async function, returns Future<void>
```

Best practice for validators:

```dart
typedef FieldValidator = String? Function(String? value);
```

dart-enums:
## 1. Basic Enum

```dart
enum UserRole {
  admin,
  manager,
  user,
}
```

Usage:

```dart
final role = UserRole.admin;

print(role.name);  // admin
print(role.index); // 0
```

---

## 2. Enhanced Enum with Value

```dart
enum OrderStatus {
  pending('Pending'),
  processing('Processing'),
  completed('Completed'),
  cancelled('Cancelled');

  final String title;

  const OrderStatus(this.title);
}
```

Usage:

```dart
final status = OrderStatus.pending;

print(status.title); // Pending
```

---

## 3. Enum with Method

```dart
enum PaymentStatus {
  paid,
  unpaid,
  failed;

  bool get isSuccess => this == PaymentStatus.paid;

  String get message {
    switch (this) {
      case PaymentStatus.paid:
        return 'Payment successful';
      case PaymentStatus.unpaid:
        return 'Payment pending';
      case PaymentStatus.failed:
        return 'Payment failed';
    }
  }
}
```

Usage:

```dart
final status = PaymentStatus.paid;

print(status.isSuccess); // true
print(status.message);   // Payment successful
```

---

## 4. Enum with API Value

Useful for REST API request/response.

```dart
enum ProductType {
  printer('printer'),
  scanner('scanner'),
  attendance('attendance');

  final String apiValue;

  const ProductType(this.apiValue);
}
```

Usage:

```dart
final type = ProductType.printer;

print(type.apiValue); // printer
```

---

## 5. Convert String to Enum

```dart
enum DeviceType {
  bluetooth,
  wifi,
  usb,
  unknown;

  static DeviceType fromValue(String value) {
    return DeviceType.values.firstWhere(
      (type) => type.name == value,
      orElse: () => DeviceType.unknown,
    );
  }
}
```

Usage:

```dart
final type = DeviceType.fromValue('wifi');

print(type); // DeviceType.wifi
```

---

## 6. Enum for UI

```dart
enum AppThemeMode {
  light('Light Mode'),
  dark('Dark Mode'),
  system('System Default');

  final String label;

  const AppThemeMode(this.label);
}
```

Usage:

```dart
DropdownButton<AppThemeMode>(
  value: AppThemeMode.system,
  items: AppThemeMode.values.map((mode) {
    return DropdownMenuItem(
      value: mode,
      child: Text(mode.label),
    );
  }).toList(),
  onChanged: (value) {},
)
```

---

## 7. Quick Memory

```dart
enum Status {
  active('Active'),
  inactive('Inactive');

  final String label;

  const Status(this.label);

  bool get isActive => this == Status.active;
}
```

Main idea:

```dart
Enhanced enum = enum + value + method + getter
```

Best for:

```dart
API status
User roles
Payment status
Device type
App theme
Order status
```

Unknown API values should produce an explicit unknown state or a validation error. Do not silently treat an unsupported device as Bluetooth.

dart-late:
### 1. Use `late final` for controllers

```dart
late final TextEditingController _nameController;

@override
void initState() {
  super.initState();
  _nameController = TextEditingController();
}

@override
void dispose() {
  _nameController.dispose();
  super.dispose();
}
```

Use for:

```dart
TextEditingController
AnimationController
ScrollController
FocusNode
PageController
TabController
```

---

### 2. Use `late final` for `AnimationController`

```dart
late final AnimationController _animationController;

@override
void initState() {
  super.initState();

  _animationController = AnimationController(
    vsync: this,
    duration: const Duration(milliseconds: 300),
  );
}

@override
void dispose() {
  _animationController.dispose();
  super.dispose();
}
```

Class must have:

```dart
with SingleTickerProviderStateMixin
```

---

### 3. Use `late final` for one-time services

```dart
late final EmployeeApiService _employeeApiService;

@override
void initState() {
  super.initState();
  _employeeApiService = EmployeeApiService();
}
```

Better if no `context` needed:

```dart
late final EmployeeApiService _employeeApiService = EmployeeApiService();
```

---

### 4. Real lazy init

Runs only when first used:

```dart
late final RegExp _emailRegex = RegExp(
  r'^[\w-\.]+@([\w-]+\.)+[\w-]{2,4}$',
);
```

Good for expensive/helper objects:

```dart
late final DateFormat _dateFormat = DateFormat('yyyy-MM-dd');
late final NumberFormat _moneyFormat = NumberFormat.currency();
late final RegExp _macRegex = RegExp(r'^([0-9A-Fa-f]{2}_){5}[0-9A-Fa-f]{2}$');
```

---

### 5. Do not use `late` for async data

Bad:

```dart
late Employee employee;

Future<void> loadEmployee() async {
  employee = await api.fetchEmployee();
}
```

Risk:

```
LateInitializationError
```

Project-safe way:

```dart
Employee? _employee;
bool _isLoading = true;

Future<void> _loadEmployee() async {
  final employee = await api.fetchEmployee();

  if (!mounted) return;

  setState(() {
    _employee = employee;
    _isLoading = false;
  });
}
```

---

### 6. Lists: avoid `late List`

Bad:

```dart
late List<Employee> employees;
```

Good:

```dart
List<Employee> _employees = [];
bool _isLoading = true;
```

---

### 7. Strings/ids from API or SharedPreferences

Bad:

```dart
late String macId;
late String deviceName;
```

Good:

```dart
String _macId = '';
String _deviceName = '';
bool _isLoading = true;
```

or:

```dart
String? _macId;
String? _deviceName;
```

---

### 8. Best rule for your project

```
Controller / FocusNode / AnimationController → late final
API / DB / SharedPreferences data → nullable or default + loading
One-time helper object → late final with initializer
Value that can be missing → never late, use ?
```

---

### 9. Common project pattern

```dart
class _MyScreenState extends State<MyScreen>
    with SingleTickerProviderStateMixin {
  late final TextEditingController _searchController;
  late final AnimationController _animationController;
  late final EmployeeApiService _employeeApiService;

  List<Employee> _employees = [];
  bool _isLoading = true;

  @override
  void initState() {
    super.initState();

    _searchController = TextEditingController();
    _animationController = AnimationController(
      vsync: this,
      duration: const Duration(milliseconds: 300),
    );
    _employeeApiService = EmployeeApiService();

    _loadEmployees();
  }

  Future<void> _loadEmployees() async {
    final employees = await _employeeApiService.fetchEmployees();

    if (!mounted) return;

    setState(() {
      _employees = employees;
      _isLoading = false;
    });
  }

  @override
  void dispose() {
    _searchController.dispose();
    _animationController.dispose();
    super.dispose();
  }
}
```

---

### 10. Final memory rule

```
late final = I will create it once, safely.
late = risky, avoid unless you are 100% sure.
async data = never trust late.
```

dart-constructors:
## `const constructor`

**Use:** Compile-time constants, immutable objects, widget optimization

### Syntax

```dart
class User {
  final String name;

  const User(this.name);
}
```

Use:

```dart
const user = User("Sony");
```

---

## Rules of `const`

✅ All fields must be `final`

```dart
class User {
  final String name;

  const User(this.name);
}
```

❌ Invalid:

```dart
class User {
  String name;

  const User(this.name); // Error
}
```

---

## Memory Reuse

```dart
const a = User("Sony");
const b = User("Sony");

print(identical(a, b));
```

Output:

```dart
true
```

Same object reused.

---

## Flutter Widget Optimization

```dart
const Text("Hello");
```

Better:

```dart
Column(
  children: const [
    Text("A"),
    Text("B"),
  ],
);
```

Benefit:

- Less rebuild work
- Better performance
- Immutable widgets

---

## `factory constructor`

**Use:** Singleton, parsing, caching, custom object creation

Syntax:

```dart
class User {
  final String name;

  User(this.name);

  factory User.guest() {
    return User("Guest");
  }
}
```

Use:

```dart
var user = User.guest();
```

---

## `factory fromMap`

Model parsing:

```dart
class User {
  final String name;

  User(this.name);

  factory User.fromMap(
      Map<String, dynamic> map) {
    return User(
      map['name'],
    );
  }
}
```

Use:

```dart
var user =
    User.fromMap(json);
```

---

## Singleton Example

```dart
class ApiService {
  static final ApiService _instance =
      ApiService._internal();

  factory ApiService() {
    return _instance;
  }

  ApiService._internal();
}
```

Use:

```dart
var a = ApiService();
var b = ApiService();

print(identical(a, b));
```

Output:

```dart
true
```

Only one instance exists.

---

## Hide Constructor + Factory

```dart
class User {
  final String name;

  User._(this.name);

  factory User.fromMap(
      Map<String, dynamic> map) {
    return User._(
      map['name'],
    );
  }
}
```

Private constructor:

```dart
User._()
```

External code:

```dart
User.fromMap(...)
```

